    <p class="gb-read" id="gbSturdyRead"></p>
    <h3>Has bitcoin been this low against gold before?</h3>
    <p class="gb-read" id="gbEarlier"></p>
    <p id="gbTwoReadings">Two readings fit the chart, and the page does not choose between them. One is that bitcoin, measured in gold, has fallen unusually far below a trend it has always returned to. The other is that gold&rsquo;s run reflects something new, such as the heavy buying by central banks since 2022 (more than 1,000 tonnes a year in each of 2022, 2023 and 2024, by the <a href="https://www.gold.org/goldhub/research/gold-demand-trends" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Gold Council</a>&rsquo;s count), and the fitted gold trend understates where gold now sits. A trend that has been tested over sixteen years of bitcoin&rsquo;s record has not yet been tested against a gold market like this one.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- §5c DID AN EARLY FIT PREDICT TODAY? (2026-10-20): out of sample, both units -->
  <section class="gb-section" id="early-fit">
    <h2>Did an early fit predict today?</h2>
    <p>The buttons at the top of the page move where the fit <em>starts</em>. This section asks a different question, the one The Power Law page uses to test the dollar trend: if the line had been fitted only to the data up to a cutoff, would it have pointed to where today&rsquo;s trend is? Here every fit starts at the first sample (<span id="gbOosFirst">&mdash;</span>), as on <a href="/the-power-law#oosChart">The Power Law</a>, ends at the cutoff you choose, and is carried forward to today. Everything after the cutoff is a test the line never saw.</p>
  </section>

