  <section class="gb-section">
    <p>Why the two units part: a dollar is the unit bitcoin&rsquo;s price is quoted in, while an ounce of gold is itself a price that moves. For the cutoff you chose, gold went from <span id="gbOosGoldCut">&mdash;</span> an ounce at the end of <span id="gbOosGoldCutYear">&mdash;</span> to <span id="gbOosGoldNow">&mdash;</span> today, and a line fitted to ounces up to the cutoff could not know that. The dollar trend is the one that has held out of sample; the gold trend is, in effect, that trend divided by whatever gold does next. The <a href="#gbReadout">&ldquo;Bitcoin cheap, or gold expensive?&rdquo;</a> card above is the same fact, read today.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- §6 GOLD AND BITCOIN AS MONEY -->
  <section class="gb-section gb-pointer">
    <h2>Gold and bitcoin, as money</h2>
    <p>This page measures one against the other. Why they are compared at all, and how they differ as money, is the subject of <a href="/the-bitcoin-migration#gold">The Bitcoin Migration&rsquo;s section on gold</a>: why gold&rsquo;s scarcity carried it for thousands of years, and why its need for custodians became its undoing. <a href="/the-half-life">The Half-Life</a> compares their supply: gold&rsquo;s grows about 1.7% a year; bitcoin&rsquo;s is fixed at 21 million.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- METHOD -->
  <div class="gb-caveats">
    <h3>Method and sources</h3>
    <p><strong>Gold:</strong> the World Bank&rsquo;s monthly gold price (Commodity Price Data, the &ldquo;Pink Sheet&rdquo;), the average of daily rates in each month, July 2010 to <span id="gbGoldLastKey">&mdash;</span>. A monthly average smooths gold&rsquo;s moves within the month, so a single month&rsquo;s reading can differ from one taken on a given day.</p>
    <p><strong>Bitcoin:</strong> the site&rsquo;s price record (the same samples behind <a href="/the-power-law">The Power Law</a>), each divided by gold&rsquo;s average for its month. <strong>Today:</strong> the live bitcoin price over the live gold price (Coinbase&rsquo;s gold spot quote, else PAX Gold, a token redeemable for one ounce); if either is unavailable, its latest monthly figure, and the line under the readout says which.</p>
    <p><strong>The trend:</strong> a least-squares fit of the logarithm of ounces on the logarithm of days since the genesis block (3 January 2009), from January of the start year you choose (2011 by default; 2013, 2016 or 2020), refitted from the data each time the page loads. It is this page&rsquo;s fit, not the site&rsquo;s dollar reference model, so the dollar multiple here (fitted the same way, for a like-for-like comparison) differs a little from the one on <a href="/the-power-law">The Power Law</a>. The usual range is the 5th to 95th percentile of the monthly multiples.</p>
    <p><strong>Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.worldbank.org/en/research/commodity-markets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Bank Commodity Markets</a>; <a href="https://www.porkopolis.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Porkopolis Economics</a>; <a href="https://stephenperrenod.substack.com/p/bitcoin-power-law-vs-gold-mid-2024" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stephen Perrenod, &ldquo;Bitcoin power law vs. gold&rdquo; (2024)</a>; <a href="https://b1m.io/gold" target="_blank" rel="noopener">b1m.io, Bitcoin vs Gold</a>.</p>
  </div>

